  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals clear skies across da \nYoop save for retreating areas of stratus/fog hugging the western \nlakeshores and floating in the open waters east of the Apostles. \nPatchy fog may linger overnight but isn't expected to penetrate\ninland overnight. Temps fall into the 50s tonight under calm \nwinds. Building high pressure will keep largely quiet weather in\ncheck for another day, however, lake breeze driven showers and \nstorms may be seen across the central UP's golden triangle \ntomorrow afternoon/evening where lake breezes off of Lake \nSuperior and Michigan collide. Confidence in this scenario is \nlow given dry air seen in area bufkit soundings, but meager \nelevated instability and enough forcing from the lake breezes \nmay be enough to kick off some precip. Have added low end PoPs \n(15% chance) in the afternoon/evening for this possibility. The\nflattening out of the persistent upper level ridge and \nreintroduction of zonal flow aloft will allow shortwave activity\nto translate into the Great Lakes later this week into the \ncoming weekend. PoPs increase Thursday ahead of a shortwave \nrounding into the Upper Midwest. Additionally, dewpoints in the \nupper 60s return Thursday, making for a muggy evening with \napparent temps pushing the upper 80s to low 90s. A weak cold \nfront then pushes through late Thursday through early Friday, \nbringing another potential round of showers and thunderstorms \ninto the weekend. While sufficient instability will be present \nfor thunderstorms, bulk shear appears to be lacking to yield \nsevere weather potential at this time. Quieter and warm \nconditions return for early next week as ridging once again \nreturns to the region.",
